# .vscodeignore — what `vsce package` leaves OUT of the .vsix.
#
# This file has no sibling to copy from: the three apps ship through Xcode
# and Gradle, which decide what goes into a bundle from the project file.
# Here the rule is inverted — everything in the working tree ships unless
# it is named below — so a missing line means a 60 MB VSIX full of
# `node_modules`, and an over-eager line means an extension that installs
# and then cannot find its own engines. Every exclusion therefore carries
# its reason, in the habit of the Android repo's .gitignore.
#
# What must survive this file, and why:
#   dist/**       the two esbuild bundles — the extension host entry point
#                 named by package.json `main`.
#   media/**      the icon, the preview script and stylesheet, and the
#                 whole of `media/rich/` — the vendored engines are read
#                 from disk at run time, so every byte of them must ship.
#   language/**   grammars and language configurations, referenced from
#                 package.json `contributes` by path.
#   package.json, README.md, CHANGELOG.md, LICENSE, PRIVACY.md
# Verify after any change with `npx vsce ls`, which prints the exact
# manifest the .vsix will carry.

# TypeScript sources. Everything a user runs is bundled into dist/, so
# shipping src/ would double the package and ship it twice over.
src/**
**/*.ts

# Tests, fixtures and golden files. They are the proof the renderer is
# byte-identical to the apps, not something an installed extension needs.
test/**
fixtures/**

# Source maps. They only serve a debugger attached to this working tree,
# and dist/extension.js.map alone is larger than the code it maps.
**/*.map

# Development dependencies. There are no runtime ones — package.json has
# no `dependencies` block at all — so the whole tree can go.
node_modules/**
package-lock.json

# Build configuration. The .vsix is already built by the time it matters.
tsconfig.json
esbuild.mjs
vitest.config.*
.editorconfig
.eslintrc*
eslint.config.*

# Repository plumbing, meaningful on GitHub and nowhere else.
.github/**
.gitignore
.gitattributes
CODEOWNERS

# Marketplace listing copy. It is the *source* of the listing, kept beside
# the code it describes; shipping it inside the artefact it describes
# would be circular.
marketplace/**

# The port specifications. They belong to the repository's history, not to
# a user's extensions folder, and they are larger than the extension.
specs/**

# Editor and test-runner scratch. `.vscode/` is tracked in git on purpose
# (it is how a contributor launches an Extension Development Host) but an
# installed extension has no use for a launch configuration.
.vscode/**
.vscode-test/**
.vscode-test.*

# A previously packaged build, so a .vsix never ends up inside a .vsix.
*.vsix

# Deliberately NOT excluded, though both are tempting:
#   media/rich/fonts/*.woff and *.ttf — every VS Code webview is Chromium
#     and takes the woff2 that each @font-face lists first, so the older
#     two formats are ~940 KB of dead weight here. They stay because the
#     vendored tree is byte-identical in all four repos and trimming it
#     for one platform is a family decision, not a packaging one.
#   media/rich/plantuml.js — 7 MB, and the single largest file in the
#     package. There is no smaller build: it has no npm distribution and
#     is vendored verbatim.

# Local scratch.
.DS_Store
.claude/**
CLAUDE.md
